We start at 1. We want to reach $N$.
If we are at square $x$, we can reach any square $y \in [x+A, x+B]$ provided $y$ is not bad.
If $y$ is bad, we cannot land on it.
Since $B$ is small, from any square $x$, we can reach a set of squares within distance $B$.
If there is a contiguous block of bad squares of length $\ge B$, say from $S$ to $S+L-1$ where $L \ge B$, then if we are at $x < S$, we can jump at most to $x+B$. If $x+B < S$, we can jump over the bad block. But if $x+B \ge S$, we might land on a bad square.
Actually, if there is a block of bad squares of length $\ge B$, can we jump over it?
Suppose we are at $x$. We can jump to $x+A, x+A+1, \dots, x+B$.
If all these potential landing spots are bad, we are stuck.
If there is a sequence of bad squares $[S, E]$ such that $E - S + 1 \ge B$, then no matter where we are at $x \le S$, the maximum reach is $x+B$. If $x+B < S$, we can jump over. If $x+B \ge S$, we might land on bad squares.
Wait, if $E - S + 1 \ge B$, does it mean we can't cross?
Suppose bad squares are $[10, 15]$. $B=5$.
If we are at 5, we can jump to $5+1=6, \dots, 5+5=10$.
If 10 is bad, we can't land on 10.
If we are at 6, we can jump to $11, \dots, 16$.
If 11 is bad, we can't land on 11.
If we are at 7, we can jump to $12, \dots, 17$.
