The problem asks us to calculate the sum of $f(P)$ over all permutations $P$ of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$, modulo 998244353.
Here, $f(A)$ is formed by concatenating the decimal representations of the elements of $A$ and interpreting the result as an integer.
Let $A = (A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_N)$. Then $f(A) = \sum_{i=1}^N A_i \cdot 10^{L_{i+1} + \dots + L_N}$, where $L_i$ is the number of digits in $A_i$. Wait, let's look at the example.
$A = (1, 20, 34)$.
$S$ starts empty.
Append "1" -> "1".
Append "20" -> "120".
Append "34" -> "12034".
So $f(A) = 1 \cdot 10^4 + 20 \cdot 10^2 + 34 \cdot 10^0$.
The number of digits in $A_i$ is $L_i$.
The term $A_i$ contributes to the sum at position determined by the number of digits following it.
Specifically, if $A_i$ is at index $i$ in the sequence $A$, it is followed by $A_{i+1}, \ldots, A_N$. The total number of digits contributed by these following elements is $\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_j$.
So $f(A) = \sum_{i=1}^N A_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_j}$.

We need to compute $\sum_{P \in S_N} f(P) \pmod{998244353}$.
Let $P = (P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N)$ be a permutation of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$.
The value is $\sum_{i=1}^N P_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.
We need to sum this over all permutations.
$\sum_{P} f(P) = \sum_{P} \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \cdot 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.
